   "history": "Ethiopia is the birthplace of coffee — the Coffea arabica plant is indigenous to the Kaffa region of southwestern Ethiopia, where it was first discovered and consumed, according to the legendary story of the goatherd Kaldi who noticed his goats dancing after eating coffee berries. The Ethiopian coffee ceremony (Buna Tetu — literally 'drink coffee') is one of the world's most elaborate and socially significant food rituals. It takes 1—2 hours from green bean to cup, involves the roasting of green beans in a pan over charcoal, the grinding in a mortar, the brewing in a clay jebena (coffee pot) over coals, and the serving in three consecutive rounds — Abol (first), Tona (second), and Baraka (third, meaning 'blessing'). The ceremony must be attended from the first cup — leaving before the third cup is considered rude. It is simultaneously an invitation, a social gathering, a meditation, and a celebration of the country's greatest contribution to world civilisation.",
