   "history": "Kashata are traditional Swahili confections made from coconut, groundnuts, or both, cooked in sugar syrup until they set into brittle, sweet clusters. They have been made along the East African coast for centuries, sold at markets, celebrations, and street stalls. The name is believed to derive from Arabic, reflecting the long trade relationship between the Swahili coast and the Arabian Peninsula.",
