  "heritage": {
   "origin": "National — the traditional method of uji preparation across Kenya",
   "history": "Fermented uji is the ancestral form of Kenyan porridge — before instant fermentation was available, the standard practice was to soak and ferment grain flour overnight before cooking. The fermentation produces lactic acid that gives the porridge a pleasant, mild tanginess while also making the porridge more nutritious by breaking down anti-nutritional compounds (phytates) that inhibit mineral absorption. In Luo communities, fermented uji (called \"uji wa ugali\" or simply \"uji\") is considered the proper traditional preparation.",
   "traditionalUtensils": [
    "Clay fermenting pot",
    "Wooden stirring stick"
   ],
   "modernEvolution": "Fermented uji is less commonly made today due to the convenience of unfermented uji. However, there is growing appreciation of its superior nutritional profile and distinctive, pleasantly tangy flavour."
  },
  "desc": "Kenyan Fermented Uji — grain flour (millet, sorghum, or maize) fermented overnight to develop a pleasant tanginess, then cooked into a smooth, nutritious porridge. The ancestral form of Kenyan uji — more flavourful and significantly more nutritious than the unfermented version.",
  "longDesc": "Fermented uji has a character that unfermented porridge cannot replicate — a pleasant, mild sourness that makes it more interesting and, scientifically speaking, significantly more nutritious. The fermentation breaks down phytic acid in the grains, dramatically increasing the bioavailability of iron, zinc, and calcium. This is the breakfast that has sustained the healthiest communities in Kenya for millennia.",
  "ingredients": [
   "1 cup mixed grain flour — finger millet, sorghum, and maize (equal parts)",
   "4 cups water — divided",
   "2 tbsp natural yogurt or whey — as a fermentation starter",
   "2—3 tbsp sugar — to taste",
   "½ cup milk — to finish",
   "Pinch of salt"
  ],
  "equipment": [
   "Bowl or clay pot for fermentation",
   "Medium saucepan",
   "Wooden spoon"
  ],
  "steps": [
   "Night before: mix flour with 2 cups of water and the yogurt/whey starter until smooth.",
   "Cover loosely and leave at room temperature for 12—24 hours until pleasantly tangy.",
   "Morning: bring remaining 2 cups of water to a boil.",
   "Stir the fermented slurry well. Pour into the boiling water in a steady stream, stirring constantly.",
   "Cook over medium heat for 8—10 minutes until smooth and thick.",
   "Add sugar and salt. Stir.",
   "Finish with milk. Serve immediately."
  ],
  "chefTips": [
   "The longer the fermentation (up to 24 hours), the more tangy the result — find your preferred balance.",
   "The yogurt/whey starter introduces beneficial lactic acid bacteria — or simply leave it out and rely on wild fermentation (takes slightly longer).",
   "Fermented uji smells pleasantly sour — like mild yogurt. If it smells unpleasant or develops mould, discard."
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
   "Fermenting in a sealed container — the fermentation needs some air circulation.",
   "Over-fermenting — more than 24 hours at room temperature can make it too sour."
  ],
  "faqs": [
   {
    "q": "Is fermented uji safe?",
    "a": "Yes — lactic acid fermentation is one of the oldest and safest food preservation methods. The lactic acid produced creates an acidic environment that inhibits harmful bacteria. This is the same process used to make yogurt, kefir, and sourdough."
   }
  ],
  "cookingScience": "Fermentation by lactic acid bacteria breaks down phytic acid (an anti-nutrient in grains that binds minerals). Studies show that fermented porridge has up to 5 times higher iron and zinc bioavailability than unfermented porridge — making it significantly more nutritious even with the same ingredients.",
  "sustainabilityTips": [
   "Fermentation requires no additional energy beyond the initial cooking — a sustainable process.",
   "Natural wild fermentation requires no commercial starter cultures."
  ],
