   "origin": "Zanzibar and the Swahili coast — one of Tanzania's most ancient breakfast foods",
   "history": "Vitumbua are small, round, slightly crispy on the outside and soft within — made from fermented rice flour and coconut milk. They are cooked in a special cast iron pan with rounded indentations (similar to an aebleskiver pan) that gives them their characteristic round shape. They have been made on the Tanzanian coast for centuries, sold by street vendors before dawn and eaten with chai. Vitumbua represent one of the oldest Swahili food traditions.",
